Your data is your moat. Build the best AI while we handle the infrastructure, whether you need 8B or 1T parameters, 1k or 1M context, LoRA or full parameter tuning, and various training methods like SFT, DPO, or custom solutions. Fireworks Training provides full flexibility without compromises.

@FireworksAI_HQ: "Fireworks Training is now in preview. You can now full-parameter fine-tune Kimi K2.5 (1T params, 256k context) with custom loss functions (GRPO, DRO, DAPO, or bring your own) on managed infrastructure. @genspark_ai built their proprietary model stack in four weeks. @vercel hit 93% error-free generation with RFT. @cursor_ai runs their RL rollout fleet on Fireworks. Full-parameter from 8B to 1T. Multi-LoRA serving. Managed or bring your own training loop. Your model is your product. Your data is your moat."

Fireworks Training allows users to fine-tune and deploy state-of-the-art, open-source LLMs and image models quickly and cost-effectively.

We didn’t ship DeepSeek V4 on Day 0 like we always do. Why? We love speed at @FireworksAI_HQ, but quality is more important than speed. Running our extensive evaluations, we found that even the official reference model code was producing corrupted outputs. When we tested over the weekend, all endpoints except the official DeepSeek API had these issues.

After two days of extensive debugging with @deepseek_ai, @sgl_project, and @vllm_project communities, the issues are fixed and we’re proud to serve DeepSeek V4 Pro in all its glory.

@FireworksAI_HQ: "DeepSeek V4 Pro is one of the most important open-model releases this year, with real advances in long-context reasoning, agentic performance, and inference efficiency."

Heard about prompt injection? Most open-source LLM API endpoints are vulnerable to special text like '<|im_end|>', which can override the system prompt and disrupt your product. We built an interactive demo to show how this happens and how we fixed it for all models at @FireworksAI_HQ.

@FireworksAI_HQ: "Prevent prompt injection. safe_tokenization: true. Keep your system yours."

The linked blog highlights a critical prompt injection vulnerability in LLM serving stacks, where user input containing model control tokens can override system prompts. Standard tokenizers often merge chat templates and user text into a single string, allowing attackers to manipulate turn boundaries or system instructions.

These models are independently evaluated by @Kimi_Moonshot. At Fireworks, attention is paid to small details like numerics, prompt formatting, and tool parsing to ensure peak quality and speed.

Huge thanks to Kimi friends for working with the community to ensure the best quality. KVV is excellent.

@iScienceLuvr: "Moonshot AI has collated a list of the Kimi K3 vendors with comparison to the official API. Only @modal and @FireworksAI_HQ have submitted full results. It seems like Fireworks is the closest."

